
Frequently Asked Questions
--------------------------

-> Why this horrible name?

It's a play on words: WebDAV - DAV - caDAVer. Gettit?


-> Why is this fork called cadaver-windows?

Because that is what it is: cadaver, ported to build and run on
Windows as a native program. The name says what you get rather than
being clever about it. See README.md.


-> Does it need MSYS2, Cygwin or WSL to run?

No. A released binary is statically linked and depends only on DLLs
that ship with Windows. MSYS2 is needed to *build* it, not to run it.

If you build it yourself without STATIC=1 the result does need the
OpenSSL, expat, readline and iconv DLLs from the toolchain that built
it, which means ucrt64/bin has to be on PATH. Git Bash ships its own,
older copies of some of those in /mingw64/bin; if those come first on
PATH they will be found instead and the program will not start.


-> `lcd C:\Users\me' does nothing, or the path comes out wrong.

That works in this fork. If it does not, you are running upstream
cadaver or an old build: the tokeniser used to read the backslash as
an escape character and eat it.

What still does not work is an MSYS or Git Bash path such as
/c/Users/me. cadaver is a native Windows program and knows nothing
about that translation. Convert it first:

  cygpath -w /c/Users/me


-> My uploaded file is corrupt / has grown by a few hundred bytes.

Not with this fork. Upstream cadaver on Windows opened transfers in
text mode, so every newline in an uploaded or downloaded file gained a
carriage return in front of it. Everything here is binary throughout.

If a file still comes back wrong, check whether it went through a
proxy that rewrites content, and compare the sizes that `ls' and the
local file report.


-> How do I see what is actually being sent?

  cadaver --trace=wire.log -r commands.txt URL

dumps every request and response to wire.log, tagged with the command
that issued it, so grepping the log for a command finds the exchange it
caused. Without a filename it goes to standard error. --verbose widens
it to everything neon reports.

Inside a session, `set debug http' turns the older neon trace on part
way through. Add `xml' to see the request and response bodies for
PROPFIND, PROPPATCH and LOCK, or `httpbody' to see every body.
`cleartext' also shows credentials, so do not paste that output
anywhere.


-> Why doesn't the "edit" command work with gvim / VS Code / Notepad++?

Because they fork and return immediately by default, so cadaver sees
the editor exit before you have typed anything and uploads the
unchanged file. Tell the editor to wait:

  set editor "gvim -f"
  set editor "code --wait"
  set editor "notepad++ -multiInst -nosession"

Put the line in .cadaverrc to make it permanent. Plain notepad, the
default on Windows, waits already.


-> Can I use it in a script?

Yes. The exit status is 0 if every command succeeded and the number
that failed otherwise, so a script can branch on it without reading
anything:

  cadaver -r commands.txt https://dav.example.com/path/ < /dev/null

For the detail, --json writes one object describing the whole session
to standard output and nothing else, and --trace dumps every request
and response tagged with the command that caused it.  AGENTS.md
documents the JSON as a contract.

  cadaver --json --trace=wire.log -r commands.txt URL > result.json


-> The server works with other WebDAV clients but cadaver says
   "Location does not advertise WebDAV class 1 support".

The server did not put `DAV: 1' in its response to OPTIONS. Some
otherwise working servers get this wrong. Use -t, or `set tolerant',
to carry on anyway.


-> Every LOCK fails with "Response missing activelock".

The server answered LOCK with a Content-Type that is not an XML media
type, so neon discarded the body and cadaver never saw the lock token.
That is a server bug; wsgidav 4.x is a well-known example. Nothing can
be done from the client side.


-> Is there a recursive get or put?

Yes: `rput' uploads a directory and everything under it, creating a
collection per directory, and `rget' does the reverse, creating the
local directories as it goes.

  rput C:\work\outbox uploads
  rget uploads C:\work\inbox

A collection that is already there is not an error. Either one given a
plain resource does what `put' or `get' would have done. Both stop at
a Ctrl-C.


-> Ctrl-C during a large transfer kills cadaver.

Not in this fork. The first one abandons the transfer and returns to
the prompt; a download leaves no local file behind, and a partly
uploaded resource is the server's to clean up. A second Ctrl-C during
the same transfer does end the session, which is the way out of one
that has stalled completely.

Ctrl-C at the prompt ends the session, as it always did.


-> How fast is my server?

  dav:/dav/> bench

writes a generated payload into the current collection, reads it back
and deletes it, and reports the round trip time of a PROPFIND and the
rate each direction moved at. `bench 4M 10' changes the payload size
and the number of repetitions. Rates are MiB/s and durations are wall
clock, so they include server and network time.

It writes to cadaver-bench.dat in the current collection, so point it
at somewhere you are willing to have written to. A resource already
under that name is left alone and the command refuses.


-> A file whose name is not in my code page cannot be uploaded.

That works in this fork. The executable carries an application
manifest that makes UTF-8 the process code page, so the narrow Windows
and C runtime calls read the UTF-8 paths cadaver hands them. Windows
10 version 1903 and later honour it.

An older Windows ignores the manifest, and there a path with a
character the active ANSI code page cannot hold still fails.


-> How do I change what `ls' prints?

  dav:/dav/> set lsformat "%T %-30n %8S %D"

A conversion is a %, an optional - for left alignment, an optional
field width, and one letter: %n the name, %h the full path, %s the
size in bytes, %S the size rounded in powers of 1024, %d the
modification time in local form, %D the same as ISO 8601 UTC, %t the
Coll: marker, %T the type as a word, %e the executable flag, %v the
version-control state, %% a literal %.

`describe lsformat' prints the same list, and `unset lsformat' returns
to the default.
